From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erik Bourget <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 02:10:16 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 45/93] arm64: dts: apple: t6050: describe the single-die
 system cache controller

Single-die T6050 boards all include t6050-max.dtsi. M5 Pro parts enable
four of the eight die-0 AMCCs (AMCC 0..3, 16 planes, amcc-enable-mask
0xf), while M5 Max ships in both 460 GB/s (binned) and 614 GB/s (eight
AMCCs, 32 planes, amcc-enable-mask 0xff) variants under the same board
device trees.

Describe the die-0 memory cache controller (apple,t6050-mcc) in
t6050-max.dtsi with the four AMCCs (16 planes) verified across both M5
Pro and M5 Max and apple,boot-dma-devices = <&dcp>, so neither M5 Pro
nor a binned M5 Max reads unverified AMCC 4..7 apertures. Size the
parent bus ranges for all eight die-0 AMCCs so the boot loader can
populate the full enabled mask from the live ADT.

Add display = <&dcp> to the boot framebuffer node on every single-die
T6050 laptop (J714c, J714s, J716c, J716s, and the K114/K116 base) so
apple-mcc waits for the internal DCP to retire the boot scanout before
enabling cache allocation, including when the bootloader keeps the boot
framebuffer and leaves the DCP disabled. Desktop boards (t6050-j775c.dts
and t6050-j873s.dts) also inherit the MCC node, have no boot framebuffer,
and keep &dcp disabled, so they stay in safe probe deferral with no cache
allocation until the boot loader enables dcp.

Signed-off-by: Erik Bourget <address-in-upstream-commit@localhost>
